@meta
id: 13-02
title: Anything in between
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-quantifiers
par: 36
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+F for the id, Enter, Enter, Enter, Enter, Enter
open: app.log

@brief
This is the log excerpt for ticket PAY-731. Order `ord_9125` timed out
at the gateway, a retry was scheduled, and the retry never fired. The
line that says the retry was scheduled needs tagging so the write-up can
point at it: append ` # PAY-731` to it.

Two obvious searches, both wrong. `/retry` finds a retry for a different
order. `/9125` finds the order's first line, ten lines before the one
you want, and `n` walks you down through the whole lifecycle to get
there.

What you actually know is that the line contains `retry` and then, some
way further along, `9125`. A pattern can say exactly that, with the gap
left unspecified.

@keys
| `*` | in a pattern: zero or more of whatever came just before it |
| `.*` | any character, any number of times — "anything at all" |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Neither word is unique on its own. Both words together, in that order,
on one line, are.
--
`.` is any character and `*` means "repeat the last thing as many times
as needed, including none". `.*` is therefore a gap of any length.
--
`/retry.*9125` `Enter` lands on the one line that has both. `A` and the
tag.

@solution
/retry.*9125<Enter>  A # PAY-731<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-nine keystrokes, and one search instead of six.

`*` on its own is rarely what you want. It attaches to the atom before
it — a character, a class, a group — and means "zero or more of that".
`ab*c` matches `ac`, `abc` and `abbbbc`. The "zero" part catches people
out: `x*` matches the empty string, so it never fails, so a search for
`/foo*` will stop at `fo` in `for`. Chapter 13 gives you `\+` for "one
or more" in a few lessons' time, which is usually what you meant.

`.*` is the combination you will type constantly. It is the regex way of
saying "and then, further along". Two things on a line, in a known
order, with an unknown amount of stuff between them — that is most of
what you need from a log file, and most of what you need when a name is
common but a *pairing* is rare.

It is greedy. `.*` grabs as much as it can and gives back only what it
must, so `/order=.*request=` matches from the first `order=` to the
*last* `request=` on the line, not the nearest. On a single log line
that rarely matters. When it does, `\{-}` is the non-greedy form, and it
gets a mention later in the chapter.

